\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Internal columns intertwine the coupled operator}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Let #{k=\mathbb F_p} with #{p} odd, and choose the
algebra isomorphism of \ref{fdip-00VU}. Let #{S=k^2}
with the resulting left #{2T} action. Then #{S} is
simple and the column map gives an isomorphism
##{\Phi:C^\bullet(X;\mathcal O_k)
       \longrightarrow C^\bullet(X;S)\otimes_k k^2.}
For every link field,
##{\begin{aligned}
\Phi\extD_{U,\mathcal O_k}&=(\extD_{U,S}\otimes\bbI_{k^2})\Phi,\\
\Phi\cov_{U,\mathcal O_k}&=(\cov_{U,S}\otimes\bbI_{k^2})\Phi.
\end{aligned}}
Here the subscripts specify the coefficient module
of each \vocabk{cochain operator}{fdip-00SR}.
The two column summands each have dimension
#{2r}, with #{r=2^n|X|}; their sum has dimension #{4r}.}

\proof{
\p{Write a coefficient matrix as #{Z=(z_1,z_2)} and
let #{w_1,w_2} be the standard basis of the final
#{k^2}. At a based cell, send it to
#{z_1\otimes w_1+z_2\otimes w_2}.
Collecting columns is a linear bijection, with
inverse assembling the two columns. Applying it
independently at each cell gives #{\Phi}.}

\p{The group contains the Hurwitz basis elements, so
its matrix images span every endomorphism of #{S}.
A subspace invariant under the group is invariant
under all these endomorphisms. If it contains a
nonzero vector, an endomorphism can send that vector
to any chosen target, so the subspace is all of
#{S}. This proves simplicity. The area form
#{\epsilon(s,t)=s_1t_2-s_2t_1} is perfect and
preserved by the determinant-one group image.
Thus the inverse-transport adjoint convention
also applies to #{S}.}

\p{Every forward link multiplies each coefficient
matrix on the left by the same inverse matrix;
each backward link multiplies it by the same
direct matrix. Left multiplication acts separately
and identically on the two columns. Exterior
multiplication and contraction act on the other
tensor factor, while the site shift simply
reindexes the column-valued functions. These
identities intertwine the raising and lowering summands
separately. Adding them gives the #{\extD_U} identity and
subtracting the lowering summands gives the #{\cov_U} identity.
There are #{r} exterior-site slots and
#{2} coordinates in each column, proving the ranks.}
}
